From 59cb96952e0c809902af57ba09179b0f2d5f0ef5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andrey Stoyan <andrey.stoyan.csam@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 23 Aug 2026 03:45:59 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 25/44] refactor(chexc): one semantic form of a capability
 list, shared with the serializer

The revision made the canonical -- "semantic" -- form of a capability list
normative: a duplicate entry, or one covered by a more general entry of the same
list, is not part of the list, and every rule that compares or consumes lists works
on that form.

The checker already behaves that way, because every comparison is coverage-based
rather than element-wise, and a test now pins it: duplicate and covered entries in a
declaration, assignment in both directions between coverage-equivalent spellings,
and an override checked against a list with a redundant entry.

What is left is type identity -- '() throws (A, B) -> R' and '() throws A -> R'
intern as two types when B is covered by A. Normalizing at interning was tried and
reverted: running subtype queries while types are being formed broke 17 cases across
generics, serialization and lambda checking, and nothing observes the difference
(assignment holds both ways by subtyping, overload resolution erases the lists, and
the .cjo is normalized on the way out).

The normalizer the serializer carried moves to TypeManager, where the checker can
use it too; the exported form is that plus a stable order, so that reordering a
clause in source stays a non-change for metadata.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
